 <section class="chapter rq-research"><div class="chapter-inner"><div class="chapter-index">03 / Research + people</div><div class="lf-split"><h2 class="chapter-title">Emergency software serves <em>different kinds of urgency.</em></h2><div class="lf-copy"><p>The brief was mapped around four operational perspectives: a resident who needs help, a volunteer deciding whether to act, an organisation managing finite supply, and an administrator protecting trust across the network.</p><p>Desk research, comparable-service review, task decomposition, persona mapping and scenario walkthroughs shaped the flows. Formal disaster-response field research remains a future requirement.</p></div></div><div class="rq-personas">${[['⌂','REQUESTER','Low bandwidth, high stress','Needs a short form, approximate location and visible response status.'],['✦','VOLUNTEER','Willing, but needs clarity','Needs urgency, distance, people affected and a safe next step.'],['▦','ORGANISATION','Capacity changes quickly','Needs inventory, assignment and fulfilment in one operating view.'],['◇','ADMIN','Trust is operational work','Needs verification, broadcasts, moderation and a clear record.']].map((x,i)=>`<article class="reveal" style="transition-delay:${i*70}ms"><i>${x[0]}</i><span>${x[1]} / ${x[2]}</span><h3>${x[3]}</h3></article>`).join('')}</div></div></section>

